\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 ABSTRACT}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum
\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 This study explores the rhetoric used by Andrew Tate, known as a \u8216 misogyny influencer\u8217 , and its impact on his followers. Tate released a vlog in response to his ban from social media. This vlog was analyzed along with his followers\u8217  comments (n\u8201 =\u8201 300) on the video. Through hegemonic masculinity and social identity frameworks, we offer a thematic analysis of Tate's rhetoric. Results contribute to the growing body of literature on online manospheric communities and why followers may be drawn to Tate's leadership styles. Findings also extend literature regarding themes that may appear in other radicalized spaces.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 FULL TEXT}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Because emerging media\u8212 specifically, social media platforms with video-sharing capabilities such as TikTok, X/Twitter, YouTube, and Instagram Reels\u8212 make it possible for the everyday person to go viral, the spread of harmful rhetoric and misinformation continues to be a growing concern in nearly every area of private and public life (Allcott & Gentzkow, 2017; Sanford et al., 2023; Suarez-Lledo & Alvarez-Galvez, 2021). For example, Russian agents hacking into federal U.S. systems to interfere with Donald J. Trump's 2016 Presidential campaign (FBI, n.d.) encouraged mis- and disinformation to be shared on social media platforms to Trump's benefit (Allcott & Gentzkow, 2017). In the years since, increased tensions have continued to arise in private groups and forums of radicalized groups gather to discuss white supremacy and hypermasculinity (e.g., Proud Boys, see Kutner, 2020; Stormfront, see Bowman-Grieve, 2009), further reinforcing harmful rhetoric and misinformation in increasingly mainstream spheres; no longer is this dangerous rhetoric resigned to dark web spaces.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 The purpose of this study is to examine the rhetoric used by social media influencer, Andrew Tate, and its impact on vulnerable audiences. Tate, who was infamously banned for his misogynistic online discourses (Sung, 2022b; Van Boom, 2022), and his followers\u8217  online messages are examined through the lens of hegemonic masculinity and social identity theoretical frameworks in an effort to add to extant literature surrounding \u8220 networked misogyny,\u8221  or the contemporary wave of online hostility directed at women (Banet-Weiser & Miltner, 2016; Bratich & Banet-Weiser, 2019; Dickel & Evolvi, 2023). We offer a rich thematic analysis of a YouTube video posted by Tate regarding his ban from the platform and the comments on this video, which was later removed. The findings from this study contribute to the body of literature surrounding the identity of online, extremist communities and why followers may be drawn to this leadership style. The results of our analysis extend the existing literature regarding online manosphere communities\u8217  rhetoric seeping into more mainstream areas such as YouTube (see also Ging, 2023; Haslop et al., 2024), as well as common gendered themes that may appear in other radicalized, online spaces which scholars aim to understand.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Literature Review}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Who is Andrew Tate? A Rundown}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj
\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Prior to the 37-year-old's rise to fame, British-American social media influencer and internet personality Andrew Tate worked as a professional kickboxer and competed internationally (Cobra Tate, n.d.; Das, 2022b). Postretirement, he appeared as a guest on the hit television show, }
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Big Brother,}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2
\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2  but was removed from the show after a video emerged of him beating a woman with a belt, along with several homophobic and racist tweets (Das, 2022b; Dickson, 2022; Holpuch, 2022). Although his estimated net worth remains unclear, Tate frequently claimed that a substantial portion of his \u8220 hundreds of millions of dollars\u8221  stemmed from an adult web-camming website he cocreated with his brother, Tristan (Das, 2022b; Dickson, 2022; Richards et al., 2022). Tate asserted that the site featured approximately 75 webcam models, which he described as his girlfriends, though seven women came forward accusing the brothers of sexual exploitation, rape, battery, and human trafficking (Chappell, 2023; Das, 2022b; Dickson, 2022; Sommer, 2022). Romanian prosecutors have since indicted the brothers for \u8220 human trafficking and forming an organized criminal group\u8221  (Kwai, 2023, para. 1). Tate mocked concerns about the ways he and his brother recruited cam models and whether they coerced the women into creating explicit content as evidenced by a picture he posted with the caption: \u8220 officer \u8230  I think we can all agree that bitches love to lie\u8221  (Sommer, 2022). As of October 2024, Tate remains under house arrest after the Bucharest Court of Appeal rejected prosecutors\u8217  petition to transfer Tate over to jail while he awaits his trial (Impelli, 2024). Though Tristan was placed under judicial control, restricting his communication with certain individuals involved in the case, the Bucharest court also lifted many restrictions on communication for both brothers, allowing them to, as a spokesperson for the brothers elaborated, \u8220 communicate with these alleged victims based on the close relationships they share and the children they have together\u8221  (Impelli, 2024. para. 4).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Tate also operates Hustlers University (HU), which he describes as an \u8220 online school that teaches people how to make money online\u8221  (Richards et al., 2022). More specifically, HU curriculum is allegedly presented by millionaire \u8220 professors\u8221  who make \u8220 anywhere from 10k-500k a month\u8221  and specialize in a variety of fields including: \u8220 Stock Analysis, Options Plays, Crypto Analysis, DeFi, NFTs, E-commerce, Copyrighting, Freelancing, and more,\u8221  (Hustlers University 3.0, n.d. para. 1). HU content can be accessed by anyone for a $50 monthly fee (Hustlers University 3.0, n.d.). However, HU has faced backlash, with many critics calling the site a \u8220 multi-level-marketing scheme\u8221  (MLM) as paying subscribers are asked to recruit new paying members. Through HU, Tate tapped into the appeal of \u8220 crypto investing and drop shipping,\u8221  recruiting boys as young as 13 years old to enroll in the program with the promises that they could pursue such business endeavors and \u8220 earn up to \'a310,000 a month [with] 48% commission for each person they refer\u8221  (Das, 2022b, para. 23).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Perhaps the most interesting facet of Tate's viral presence is the fact that he rarely directly promotes his own content on major platforms. Instead, subscribers are urged to capture and condense videos of HU content, specifically controversial clips, or any other public appearances made by Tate (i.e., interviews or podcasts he has participated in) and share the clips through their accounts instead (Das, 2022b; Dickson, 2022; Wilson, 2022). By doing so, Tate quickly gained traction with users; in July of 2022, \u8220 there were more Google searches for his name than for Donald Trump or Kim Kardashian\u8221  (Das, 2022b, para. 7).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Key points of Tate's rhetoric}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Tate is known for his inflammatory speech on his podcast, }{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2
\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Tate Speech}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 , which has been banned from many social media platforms for promoting hate speech (Sung, 2022a). }
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Tate Speech}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2
\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2  episodes that were released prior to the ban are still available on Apple Podcasts and are flagged under the category \u8220 Self-Improvement\u8221 ; they are described as providing Tate's \u8220 flawless\u8221  perspective on life, attracting those who are interested in Tate's network of like-minded men (Tate & Tate, 2022). On Spotify, Tate's podcast episodes and clips are all concentrated under one account that still posts regularly, and even addresses current events (Tate, 2024). Topics on Tate's podcast and social media range from economic success tips, relationship advice and gender-specific topics, health and fitness, and societal critiques of government.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Economic Success and Wealth}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Tate relies on his wealth as his primary self-promotion technique, often shaming those who do not make as much money while frequently flaunting the catchphrase \u8220 what color is your Bugatti?\u8221  to do so (Bhattacharya, 2022). On one of Tate's many websites, Cobra Tate, he writes \u8220 success is learnt,\u8221  followed by his offer to teach the \u8220 deserving\u8221  how to become wealthy (Cobra Tate, n.d.). Tate sees his wealth as something others should aspire to achieve, flaunting it as a rebuttal to many of his critics\u8217  claims. He also asserts that his extreme wealth has put him in a hierarchical place above the state, one in which he lives with armed guards and multiple passports (Richards et al., 2022). For Tate, money equals power and a right to say what you want without consequence.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Gender Roles and Relationships}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 When asked why people call him sexist, Tate argues that he \u8220 loves women [but] tells the truth about society,\u8221  a \u8220 truth\u8221  that paints women as weak, unable to \u8220 fight,\u8221  more likely to \u8220 crack under pressure\u8221 ; Tate regularly asserts that \u8220 the smartest people in the world are men\u8221  (Richards et al., 2022). He has also claimed that women in relationships belong to men (Das, 2022b; Richards et al., 2022) because they are innately subservient and \u8220 biologically\u8221  crave monogamy, whereas men are \u8220 biologically\u8221  wired to crave sex (Weeks & Gaines, 2021). Tate's assertions about gender-based violence and sexual assault are disturbing, as he consistently leans on a victim-blaming fallacy (Das, 2022b). In the investigation into the aforementioned sex trafficking charges, close attention was directed at a clip of Tate stating that Romanian rape laws would be less difficult to evade than those of the United States or United Kingdom, and \u8220 I\u8217 m not a rapist, but I like the idea of just being able to do what I want. I like being free\u8221  (Das, 2022b, para. 19). This clip quickly became the primary evidence behind the rumors that he was committing sex crimes in Romania.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Fitness and Wellness}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 HU markets a \u8220 Get Fit\u8221  program, promoting the assertion that men's happiness depends on physical fitness. This hypermasculine prescription of the idealized and standardized male body reinforces the notion that a man's worth is equated with appearance. In this way, Tate bullies men into believing they are undeserving if they do not embody this \u8220 macho\u8221  ideal of a dominant and protective man who is physically capable to protect women. In terms of \u8220 mental fitness,\u8221  Andrew Tate has discussed at length how he feels that \u8220 depression isn\u8217 t real\u8221 \u8212 that depression is a mindset fueled by your belief in its existence (Weeks & Gaines, 2021). He claims that \u8220 being a man is \u8230  taking the trauma which happens to you, and internalizing it, and using it in a way to build yourself into a stronger, more powerful man \u8230  a man who's never had trauma is not a capable man\u8221  (Richards et al., 2022).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 The \u8220 Matrix\u8221 }{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Tate has openly expressed disdain for the \u8220 matrix,\u8221  which is both a reference to the 1999 hit movie starring Keanu Reeves and the idea that societal forces aim to control and silence people within the \u8220 simulation\u8221  that makes up our lives and that one must \u8220 wake up\u8221  to acknowledge this simulation (i.e., the choice of taking the \u8220 red pill\u8221  in }
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 The Matrix}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2
\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2  film). Similarly, the concept of the matrix is also prominent among communities adjacent to Tate and his fanbase, such as those who identify as \u8220 involuntarily celibate\u8221  (i.e., incels), who describe taking an imaginary \u8220 black pill,\u8221  or waking up to the \u8220 belief that men are actually the marginalized gender\u8221  (Glace et al., 2021, p. 293; see also Ging, 2017). Tate's idea of the matrix that he urges his followers to wake up to include (but are not limited to): (a) governments who have the ability to limit personal freedoms, such as through gun control and mask requirements during the COVID-19 pandemic; (b) the idea of the nation-state, or borders preventing the flows of people; (c) the media as a force that gatekeeps and restricts certain information and viewpoints while silencing others; and (d) media/web infrastructure, such as web hosts that are able to deplatform users and websites (Dahir, 2022; Richards et al., 2022). Further insights into Tate's matrix are explored in the results section.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Banning Tate from social media}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 On Friday, August 19, 2022, Meta confirmed that Tate was permanently removed from its platforms, Facebook, and Instagram; YouTube and TikTok followed suit on Monday, August 22, 2022, banning Tate's account along with accounts found to be associated with or reposting Tate's content (Sung, 2022b). Tate had long been banned from Twitter after composing a tweet in 2017 in which he insinuated that rape victims coming forward during the #MeToo movement were partially responsible for the assaults they endured (Van Boom, 2022). However, Elon Musk, the new CEO of Twitter at the time, reinstated Tate's account and paid him $20,000 for advertising the platform to others in the alt-right sphere (Lorenz, 2023). Gradually, much of Tate's content has been removed from the most popular free, publicly accessible sites, though clips and snippets from his videos are still dispersed through fans, commentators, and former collaborators such as the }
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Fresh&Fit}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2
\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2  Podcast. Tate also closed his HU subscription program that promoted affiliate marketing shortly after the social media bans, claiming that his content was being reposted out of context (Das, 2022a).}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}
\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0
\ulnone\cf2 While the implications of social media takedowns are numerous\u8212 including free speech concerns regarding the pervasive social media use that is characteristic of many modern public figures\u8212 questions regarding how to better moderate platforms and new rules put in place to account for the development of tech giants\u8217  visions for a Web3 are also top of mind (Roose, 2022; see also Wilson, 2022). According to Roose (2022), Web3 is a \u8220 new kind of internet service that is built using decentralized blockchains\u8212 the shared ledger systems used by cryptocurrencies like BitCoin and Ether\u8221  (para. 1). Proponents of Web3 are essentially concerned with deregulating the \u8220 gatekeepers\u8221  (Roose, 2022, para. 3) of the current internet (i.e., large corporations and companies such as Google, Meta, Apple), and instead, offering a new \u8220 community-governed\u8221  internet space \u8220 owned by the builders and users, operated through a token system\u8221  (Dixon, 2021, para. 1). Since Tate previously set HU up as an MLM, the possibility that he will recreate new financial schemes that suit Web3 is plausible (Das, 2022a). Whether Tate will relocate his MLM to a new site remains to be seen, but he has relocated the bulk of his commentary content\u8212 and is distributing new content after his ban from YouTube\u8212 on Rumble, a less restrictive video site. Tate also claims that he was banned from services such as Airbnb and Uber (Yahoo!News, 2022), but it remains unclear as to whether these bans exist and the status of their permanence. It also remains uncertain which sites have officially reinstated Tate given the wide range of fan accounts posing as official pages, since fans continue to upload content of Tate and promote it through sites such as Facebook and Instagram; in short, his presence\u8212 or at the very least, his image\u8212 remains.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Following Top G: An Analysis of Tate's Subscribers}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Social identity theory seeks to explain how social categorization (belonging to a certain social group) and social comparison (comparing in-groups to out-groups) affects one's behavior (Tajfel, 1974). Social identity theory has been used to explain how members of extremist groups act as a result of group categorization and comparison (e.g., Furl, 2022). Here we can consider engaging in male supremacy\u8212 or the emergence and spread of misogynist ideologies, specifically the belief in \u8220 cisgender men's superiority and right to dominate and others\u8221  (IRMS, n.d., para. 1)\u8212 as participating in extremist group (s). More specifically, when examining online male supremacy forums, Furl (2022) found evidence that male separatists favored other members of their male supremacist in-group along with the male gender more broadly.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Similarly, Reicher et al. (2008) developed a five-step model of collective hate, using social identity theory as a lens, to explain how members of extremist groups come to know hate toward other groups as \u8220 right\u8221  (p. 1313). The first step of this process is }
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 identification}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2
\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 , in which the in-group is created (Reicher et al., 2008). Next is }{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 exclusion}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 , or the identification of the in-group's out-groups (Reicher et al., 2008). The third step is }{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 threat}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 , or the perception of these out-groups as a threat to the in-group (Reicher et al., 2008). Fourth is }
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 virtue}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0
\ulnone\cf2 , which consists of espousing the in-group as the morally correct group (Reicher et al., 2008). Finally, the fifth step is }{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0
\ulnone\cf2 celebration}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 , or the commemoration of the eradication of the out-group to protect the virtue of the in-group (Reicher et al., 2008). This model has been used to examine male supremacy groups\u8217  (i.e., men's rights groups, incels, etc.) discourses online (IRMS, n.d.; Marveggio, 2020). In the case of Tate and his followers, the in-group is nebulous and could be expansive (i.e., all people who view and subscribe to Tate's freely available and mainstreamed content) or highly specific (i.e., those who are involved in the innermost circles of Tate's paid programs); this in-group confusion makes predicting who will ultimately align with the collective hate model more difficult.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Uncertainty identity theory is closely related to social identity theory, as it seeks to explain one potential motivation for identifying with a group (Hogg, 2007). According to Hogg (2007), feelings of uncertainty related to the self are often psychologically uncomfortable, therefore, individuals are motivated to reduce their feelings of uncertainty. While there are multiple methods by which an individual can reduce their feelings of uncertainty, identifying with a group is argued to be a particularly effective means for one to do so (Hogg, 2007). Indeed, research has demonstrated that individuals are more likely to identify with a group under conditions of high uncertainty (Grant & Hogg, 2012), especially when the uncertainty involves an individual's collective self (Hogg & Mahajan, 2018). This occurs when the uncertainty originates from an individual's perception that there is a lack of distinctiveness to the group, how well an individual perceives themselves to fit into or be accepted by an important group, and/or how well an individual perceives their group to fit into their society broadly (cf. Hogg & Mahajan, 2018).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Uncertainty identity theory also argues that it is highly entitative groups that are likely to be most attractive to individuals seeking to shed their self-uncertainty (Hogg, 2007). Essentially, individuals are likely to seek out groups that are highly cohesive and well defined with shared beliefs and goals (Hogg, 2007). This attraction to highly entitative groups makes individuals more susceptible to groups that are more extremist in nature (2007), as they are more likely to provide individuals \u8220 with a self-saturating, rigidly defined, exclusionary, and highly prescriptive social identity and sense of self\u8221  (Hogg, 2020, p. 15). Consistent with this idea, research has shown that self-uncertainty increases identification with radical groups (Hogg et al., 2010). Further, there is evidence that those who feel they are not yet fully accepted by the group, but believe there is a path to ingroup acceptance, are more willing to commit or support extreme and aggressive antisocial behavior on behalf of the group (Goldman & Hogg, 2016). In the case of Andrew Tate and his followers, the perceived pathway to ingroup acceptance may be going from a subscriber who only has access to Tate's free content to someone who pays subscription fees to join Tate's private content groups such as HU, or his workout series, the War Room. Others may go deeper into the options Tate provides depending on their resources to do so.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Additionally, uncertainty identity theory asserts that individuals desire clear information on group norms (Hogg, 2020). A leader can help to create a clearly defined prototype for individuals to adhere to within a group (Hogg, 2020). Research has demonstrated that self-uncertainty creates a desire for leadership, particularly leadership that is perceived as \u8220 strong and directive and to varying degrees, authoritarian\u8221  (Hogg & Adelman, 2013, p. 445). Self-uncertainty amongst a collective of individuals appears to provide fertile ground for populism and populist leaders to emerge (Hogg, 2020). Though Tate is not exactly reminiscent of a populist leader, he does feel that he speaks to \u8220 men as a whole\u8221  (Tate's Final Message Video, 00:42:41) and frequently references how he has \u8220 come from nothing\u8221  during monologues about his upbringing. Tate is distinct from a typical populist in that he believes he is an elite figure, and that instead of uplifting the \u8220 common man,\u8221  he converts them into members of an elite in-group (Smith & Higgins, 2024). He claims to be for \u8220 every man\u8221  but only celebrates the men who buy into his lifestyle, representing a unique blend of old-fashioned plutocratic leadership (i.e., the influence of the wealthy in and on governing bodies, see Kuhner, 2015) and a dedication to recruiting low- to middle-class men into the fold of his in-group.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Support of Tate has amplified among far-right conservatives, given his outspoken support for Donald Trump, critiques of COVID-19 vaccines and mask mandates, traditional and religious values, progun rhetoric, and associations with conservative figures (Das, 2022b). Support has also originated among male supremacists, including incels, who have latched onto Tate's misogynistic messages about women and sexual autonomy. Though research regarding the links between incels and the media is still somewhat limited, a surge in popular media coverage and scholarly literature demonstrates a steady increase in understanding the connections and influences between the incel movement and media use (Byerly, 2020; Helm et al., 2024; O\u8217 Malley & Helm, 2023; Solea & Sugiura, 2023). Unlike previous supremacist movements that were established prior to the advent of social media (e.g., the KKK), the incel movement has had the luxury of a widespread, immediate reach to its members in online forum spaces (Byerly, 2020). Further, media coverage and forum commentaries on violent crimes committed by people who openly identify as incels (e.g., Elliot Rodger, a self-declared incel responsible for a series of six homicides) may have the potential to inspire similar acts and shape future discourses about the incel movement (Brunson, 2021). Media's role in communication between and about incels\u8212 and male supremacists more broadly\u8212 serves as an important focus in this study as Tate's remarks reinforce a cycle of media messages that victim-blame sexual assault survivors, shame women for their sexual activity, and purport that men have a hierarchical dominance over women (Stubbs-Richardson et al., 2018; Williamson, 2024).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Hegemonic masculinity theory and male collective narcissism}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24
\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Though the term \u8220 toxic masculinity\u8221  has made frequent appearances in academic and mainstream publications throughout the last several years, a universal definition has not yet been established (Harrington, 2021). While the term may seem to capture the dangers of gendered violence and hegemonic oppressions, it can be argued that labeling masculinities as \u8220 toxic\u8221  may be isolating or reinforce gender expectations and hierarchies rather than addressing the dangers of weaponized masculinity or gender-specific violence (Harrington, 2021). Masculinities are complex and warrant a careful, thorough examination of overlaps, contrasts, and applications rather than a generalizing label such as \u8220 toxic\u8221  (Connell & Messerschmidt, 2005).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 One productive way to explore masculinities is through the application of hegemonic masculinity as an organizing concept, or put simply, the pattern of men systematically and socially dominating nonbinary folks and women (Connell, 1987). Hegemonic masculinity can be viewed as the legitimization of inequalities in gender relations, or a series of arguments that rest on a foundational argument of \u8220 this is how it has always been\u8221 ; it occurs at global, regional, and local levels, introducing important discrepancies in women's experiences that must be considered (Messerschmidt, 2019). Importantly, Connell's (1987) conceptualization of hegemonic masculinity is not without critiques, but recent clarifications and efforts to distinguish hegemonic masculinity from the concept of }
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 dominant masculinity}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2
\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2  (see Messerschmidt, 2019) remind researchers to focus on hegemonic masculinities as part of the situational social systems that shape perceived norms. In the case of Tate, media structures that are part of our social systems could be sites for the perpetuation of hegemonic masculinities. Similarly, for incels, seeing themselves as subordinate to \u8220 Chads\u8221 \u8212 or the dominant hegemonic class of men\u8212 reinforces ideas about gender hierarchies within }
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 and}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0
\ulnone\cf2  between genders (Daly & Reed, 2022).}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Male collective narcissism (MCN) is a term used to describe a type of narcissism that occurs at the group level and through which group members express similar beliefs as an individual narcissist (e.g., superiority) for their in-group; such attitudes may result in negative attitudes toward the out-group (s) of women and nonbinary folks (Marchlewska et al., 2022). If Tate teaches men to behave like him (which he claims to do), not only is he teaching them narcissistic tendencies, but he continuously asks them to promote his content and spread his messages by sharing how he has helped them and how they feel like they belong in his HU and/or War Room communities. This kind of rhetoric could embolden the in-group and create a cycle of MCN that feeds itself and stabilizes a hegemonic masculinity. Collective narcissism has also been connected to a higher likelihood of adhering to conspiracy theories (Golec de Zavala et al., 2022), which could align with some of Tate's more outlandish theories about women, the government, and the media.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
